1
00:03:33,599 --> 00:03:39,599
Hotel Guests Check In...But They Don't Check Out!

2
00:07:07,199 --> 00:07:13,199
Tower of Terror is a comedy, family, horror film directed by D.J. MacHale.

3
00:10:40,799 --> 00:10:46,799
A disgraced reporter investigates an abandoned luxury hotel where five
people mysteriously disappeared sixty years earlier.

4
00:14:14,399 --> 00:14:20,399
Buzzy Crocker, played by Steve Guttenberg.

5
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:54,000
Anna Petterson, played by Kirsten Dunst.

6
00:21:21,599 --> 00:21:27,599
Jill Perry, played by Nia Peeples.

7
00:24:55,200 --> 00:25:01,200
Chris 'Q' Todd, played by Michael McShane.

8
00:28:28,799 --> 00:28:34,799
Abigail Gregory, played by Amzie Strickland.

9
00:32:02,399 --> 00:32:08,399
Claire Poulet, played by Melora Hardin.

10
00:35:36,000 --> 00:35:42,000
The Twilight Zone Tower of Terror, or simply Tower of Terror, is a series of similar accelerated drop tower dark rides located at
Disney's Hollywood Studios, Tokyo DisneySea, Walt Disney Studios Park, and formerly located at Disney California Adventure.

11
00:39:09,599 --> 00:39:15,599
The attraction is inspired by Rod Serling's anthology television series, The Twilight Zone, and
takes place in the fictional "Hollywood Tower Hotel" in Hollywood, California.

12
00:42:43,199 --> 00:42:49,199
The Tokyo version features an original storyline not related to The
Twilight Zone and takes place in the fictional "Hotel Hightower".

13
00:46:16,799 --> 00:46:22,799
All versions of the attraction place riders in a seemingly ordinary hotel elevator, and present a fictional backstory in which people
have mysteriously disappeared from the elevator under the influence of a supernatural element many years previously.

14
00:49:50,400 --> 00:49:56,400
The original version of the attraction opened at Disney's Hollywood Studios,
then named Disney-MGM Studios, at Walt Disney World in July 1994.

15
00:53:24,000 --> 00:53:30,000
A decade later, Disney began plans to add similar versions of the attraction to their newest parks at
the Disneyland Resort in California, Tokyo Disney Resort in Japan, and Disneyland Paris.

16
00:56:57,599 --> 00:57:03,599
In California and Paris, Disney sought to use the popular attraction to boost
attendance at the respective resorts' struggling new theme parks.

17
01:00:31,199 --> 01:00:37,199
The California and Tokyo versions of Tower of Terror opened in 2004 and 2006, respectively,
while financial problems delayed the opening of the Paris version until 2007.

18
01:04:04,799 --> 01:04:10,799
The California version closed in January 2017 and was replaced by Guardians of the Galaxy – Mission:
Breakout!, which was later incorporated into the adjacent Avengers Campus in 2021.

19
01:07:38,400 --> 01:07:44,400
The Tower of Terror buildings are among the tallest
structures found at their respective Disney resorts.

20
01:11:12,000 --> 01:11:18,000
At 199 feet (60.7 m), the Florida version is the second tallest attraction at the Walt Disney World Resort,
with only Expedition Everest at Disney's Animal Kingdom being taller by 0.5 feet (0.2 m).

21
01:14:45,599 --> 01:14:51,599
After its retheme, the 183-foot (55.8 m) structure at the Disneyland Resort is still the tallest
building on the property, as well as one of the tallest buildings in Anaheim.

22
01:18:19,199 --> 01:18:25,199
At Disneyland Paris, it is the second tallest attraction.

23
01:21:52,800 --> 01:21:58,800
The film explores themes of hotel, halloween, hollywood, ghost, elevator.

24
01:25:26,399 --> 01:25:32,399
The tone is lighthearted and dark.
